1
00:00:00,186 --> 00:00:04,161
Right now, somewhere on Earth,
someone is opening a Coca-Cola.

2
00:00:04,161 --> 00:00:06,899
And someone else. And someone else.

3
00:00:06,899 --> 00:00:10,536
Every single day, people drink
about two point two billion

4
00:00:10,536 --> 00:00:12,754
servings of the company's drinks.

5
00:00:12,754 --> 00:00:16,892
It started in 1886 with a man
selling about nine glasses a

6
00:00:16,892 --> 00:00:19,070
day... in a pharmacy.

7
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:23,207
Along the way, its owners sold one
of the most valuable ideas in

8
00:00:23,207 --> 00:00:27,357
the company's history for one
dollar, changed the recipe and got

9
00:00:27,357 --> 00:00:31,295
yelled at by an entire country,
and shipped whole bottling

10
00:00:31,295 --> 00:00:33,123
plants to a world war.

11
00:00:33,123 --> 00:00:36,636
So how did a drink that sold nine
glasses a day take over the

12
00:00:36,636 --> 00:00:41,850
planet? It starts with a wounded
soldier and a jug of syrup.

13
00:00:44,805 --> 00:00:47,918
John Pemberton was a
pharmacist in Georgia.

14
00:00:47,918 --> 00:00:52,267
In the Civil War, he was badly
wounded by a saber, and like many

15
00:00:52,267 --> 00:00:55,655
veterans of his time, he ended up
fighting an addiction to

16
00:00:55,655 --> 00:00:56,736
morphine.

17
00:00:56,736 --> 00:01:00,161
So he did what pharmacists
did in the 1880s.

18
00:01:00,161 --> 00:01:03,111
He mixed tonics and sold them as cures.

19
00:01:03,111 --> 00:01:07,423
His best seller was a coca wine:
wine mixed with an extract of

20
00:01:07,423 --> 00:01:08,849
the coca leaf.

21
00:01:08,849 --> 00:01:12,036
Then, in 1886, Atlanta went dry.

22
00:01:12,036 --> 00:01:14,721
Overnight, his best seller had a problem.

23
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:16,671
Pemberton went back to his kettle.

24
00:01:16,671 --> 00:01:20,621
He dropped the wine, kept the
coca leaf, added kola nut...

25
00:01:20,621 --> 00:01:22,084
and a lot of sugar.

26
00:01:22,084 --> 00:01:24,348
The result was a syrup.

27
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:28,786
On May 8th, 1886, he carried a jug
of it down the street to

28
00:01:28,786 --> 00:01:33,036
Jacobs' Pharmacy. There, it was
mixed with carbonated water and

29
00:01:33,036 --> 00:01:35,161
sold at the soda fountain.

30
00:01:35,161 --> 00:01:37,260
Five cents a glass.

31
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:40,798
His bookkeeper, Frank Robinson,
came up with the name,

32
00:01:40,798 --> 00:01:44,135
Coca-Cola, after two of its ingredients.

33
00:01:44,135 --> 00:01:47,510
He also wrote it out in the flowing
script the brand still uses

34
00:01:47,510 --> 00:01:48,390
today.

35
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:52,627
In the first year, sales averaged
about nine drinks a day.

36
00:01:52,627 --> 00:01:56,427
By most accounts, Pemberton spent
more on advertising than the

37
00:01:56,427 --> 00:01:57,871
drink earned.

38
00:01:57,871 --> 00:01:59,946
And he was running out of time.

39
00:01:59,946 --> 00:02:03,671
Sick and short on money, he sold
off pieces of the business to

40
00:02:03,671 --> 00:02:05,109
different partners.

41
00:02:05,109 --> 00:02:08,296
In 1888, John Pemberton died.

42
00:02:08,296 --> 00:02:11,317
He never saw what his syrup would become.

43
00:02:14,252 --> 00:02:17,490
One of those buyers was an
Atlanta businessman named Asa

44
00:02:17,490 --> 00:02:22,089
Candler. By 1891, he
controlled the whole thing.

45
00:02:22,089 --> 00:02:26,027
The total price, by the usual
count: about two thousand three

46
00:02:26,027 --> 00:02:27,528
hundred dollars.

47
00:02:27,528 --> 00:02:29,203
Candler wasn't a chemist.

48
00:02:29,203 --> 00:02:33,190
He was a salesman. And he
understood something simple: nobody

49
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:35,690
buys a drink they've never tasted.

50
00:02:35,690 --> 00:02:37,178
So he gave it away.

51
00:02:37,178 --> 00:02:41,002
The company handed out and mailed
tickets for a free glass.

52
00:02:41,002 --> 00:02:46,915
By its own later estimate, between
1894 and 1913, about one in

53
00:02:46,915 --> 00:02:50,442
nine Americans got a free
Coca-Cola this way.

54
00:02:50,442 --> 00:02:52,817
Then he made sure you
couldn't forget the name.

55
00:02:52,817 --> 00:02:57,455
It went on clocks, calendars,
serving trays, paper fans...

56
00:02:57,455 --> 00:03:01,000
anything that would hang on a
wall or sit on a counter.

57
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,775
By 1895, Coca-Cola was
sold in every state.

58
00:03:05,775 --> 00:03:09,850
And in the early 1900s, the cocaine
was taken out of the coca

59
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:11,331
leaf extract.

60
00:03:11,331 --> 00:03:14,731
Candler had built a hit at
the soda fountain.

61
00:03:14,731 --> 00:03:18,231
Now he was about to make one of the
strangest deals in business

62
00:03:18,231 --> 00:03:19,573
history.

63
00:03:22,487 --> 00:03:27,387
In 1899, two lawyers from
Chattanooga, Tennessee, came to see

64
00:03:27,387 --> 00:03:31,937
him. Benjamin Thomas and Joseph
Whitehead had an idea: put

65
00:03:31,937 --> 00:03:33,936
Coca-Cola in bottles.

66
00:03:33,936 --> 00:03:35,761
Candler wasn't impressed.

67
00:03:35,761 --> 00:03:38,786
Bottling was messy, and his
drink was made for the soda

68
00:03:38,786 --> 00:03:41,861
fountain. But if they wanted
to take the risk...

69
00:03:41,861 --> 00:03:42,748
fine.

70
00:03:42,748 --> 00:03:46,273
He sold them the rights to bottle
Coca-Cola across most of the

71
00:03:46,273 --> 00:03:49,048
United States. For one dollar.

72
00:03:49,048 --> 00:03:52,068
And, reportedly, he never
even collected it.

73
00:03:52,068 --> 00:03:54,206
That dollar built the empire.

74
00:03:54,206 --> 00:03:57,881
Thomas and Whitehead didn't build
every plant themselves.

75
00:03:57,881 --> 00:04:01,368
They signed up local businessmen who
paid for their own bottling

76
00:04:01,368 --> 00:04:05,116
plants, their own trucks and
their own salesmen.

77
00:04:05,116 --> 00:04:09,041
Town after town got its own
Coca-Cola bottler, with its own

78
00:04:09,041 --> 00:04:12,679
money on the line. The
company sold the syrup.

79
00:04:12,679 --> 00:04:14,699
The bottlers did the rest.

80
00:04:14,699 --> 00:04:17,111
But success attracts copycats.

81
00:04:17,111 --> 00:04:20,662
Soon there were drinks with almost
the same name, in almost the

82
00:04:20,662 --> 00:04:21,927
same bottle.

83
00:04:21,927 --> 00:04:26,477
So in 1915, the bottlers asked
glass makers for a bottle you

84
00:04:26,477 --> 00:04:28,440
could recognize in the dark...

85
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,614
or even broken on the ground.

86
00:04:30,614 --> 00:04:34,627
A glass company in Terre Haute,
Indiana, came back with a curvy

87
00:04:34,627 --> 00:04:37,607
design. The contour bottle.

88
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:44,795
In 1919, Candler's family sold
the company to a group of

89
00:04:44,795 --> 00:04:47,982
investors led by banker Ernest Woodruff.

90
00:04:47,982 --> 00:04:51,361
The price: twenty-five million dollars.

91
00:04:51,361 --> 00:04:54,748
A few years later, his son
Robert took over, and he ran

92
00:04:54,748 --> 00:04:56,873
Coca-Cola for decades.

93
00:04:56,873 --> 00:05:01,111
His goal was easy to say and hard
to do: put a Coke within an

94
00:05:01,111 --> 00:05:03,012
arm's reach of desire.

95
00:05:03,012 --> 00:05:06,187
That meant cartons that
carried six bottles home.

96
00:05:06,187 --> 00:05:08,262
Coolers at gas stations.

97
00:05:08,262 --> 00:05:12,350
Vending machines. And ads
everywhere, including the Christmas

98
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:16,450
paintings of Santa Claus
that started in 1931.

99
00:05:16,450 --> 00:05:19,775
And no, Coca-Cola didn't
invent the red Santa.

100
00:05:19,775 --> 00:05:22,175
He was wearing red long before that.

101
00:05:22,175 --> 00:05:26,050
But those ads made a warm, jolly
Santa the one most people

102
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:27,000
picture.

103
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,087
Then came World War Two.

104
00:05:29,087 --> 00:05:34,138
In 1941, Woodruff made a promise:
every man in uniform would get

105
00:05:34,138 --> 00:05:37,750
a bottle of Coke for five
cents, wherever he was...

106
00:05:37,750 --> 00:05:39,839
and whatever it cost the company.

107
00:05:39,839 --> 00:05:43,514
To keep that promise, Coca-Cola
shipped bottling equipment

108
00:05:43,514 --> 00:05:47,839
overseas. During the war,
sixty-four bottling plants were set

109
00:05:47,839 --> 00:05:50,883
up, as close to the fighting as possible.

110
00:05:50,883 --> 00:05:54,408
Millions of soldiers came
home loving the drink.

111
00:05:54,408 --> 00:05:57,745
And the company came out of the war
with a head start in dozens

112
00:05:57,745 --> 00:06:02,700
of countries. Coca-Cola went
global by following the army.

113
00:06:05,626 --> 00:06:08,264
Meanwhile, a rival was growing.

114
00:06:08,264 --> 00:06:12,963
Pepsi-Cola was born in North
Carolina in 1898, and it went

115
00:06:12,963 --> 00:06:14,942
bankrupt more than once.

116
00:06:14,942 --> 00:06:18,755
In those hard years, Coca-Cola
reportedly had chances to buy

117
00:06:18,755 --> 00:06:21,523
Pepsi. It passed every time.

118
00:06:21,523 --> 00:06:23,660
Pepsi fought back with price.

119
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:26,810
During the Great Depression, it sold
a twelve-ounce bottle for a

120
00:06:26,810 --> 00:06:31,817
nickel. The same price as Coke's
six-and-a-half-ounce bottle.

121
00:06:31,817 --> 00:06:33,979
Coke answered with feelings.

122
00:06:33,979 --> 00:06:38,230
In 1971, it filmed young people
from around the world on a

123
00:06:38,230 --> 00:06:42,517
hilltop in Italy, singing about
buying the world a Coke.

124
00:06:42,517 --> 00:06:46,115
A version of that song became
a hit on the radio.

125
00:06:46,115 --> 00:06:48,253
Pepsi answered with taste.

126
00:06:48,253 --> 00:06:52,740
In the Pepsi Challenge, which
started in 1975, people took a sip

127
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:54,890
from two unmarked cups...

128
00:06:54,890 --> 00:06:58,100
and on camera, a lot of them picked Pepsi.

129
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:01,175
Coke was still bigger, but
it was losing ground.

130
00:07:01,175 --> 00:07:05,163
And inside the company, someone
asked a dangerous question: what

131
00:07:05,163 --> 00:07:07,370
if we change the taste?

132
00:07:10,267 --> 00:07:14,280
Coca-Cola tested a new, sweeter
formula on nearly two hundred

133
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:16,004
thousand consumers.

134
00:07:16,004 --> 00:07:19,092
In blind tests, it beat the old Coke.

135
00:07:19,092 --> 00:07:20,788
It even beat Pepsi.

136
00:07:20,788 --> 00:07:26,488
On April 23rd, 1985, the company
announced it was replacing its

137
00:07:26,488 --> 00:07:28,801
ninety-nine-year-old recipe.

138
00:07:28,801 --> 00:07:30,913
America did not take it well.

139
00:07:30,913 --> 00:07:33,063
People stocked up on the old Coke.

140
00:07:33,063 --> 00:07:34,989
Protest groups formed.

141
00:07:34,989 --> 00:07:38,776
By June, the company's hotline was
getting about fifteen hundred

142
00:07:38,776 --> 00:07:40,440
calls a day.

143
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:43,427
The taste tests had asked
the wrong question.

144
00:07:43,427 --> 00:07:45,753
People weren't just drinking a flavor.

145
00:07:45,753 --> 00:07:48,240
They were drinking something
they grew up with.

146
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:51,790
Change it, and it felt like the
company had taken something that

147
00:07:51,790 --> 00:07:53,170
belonged to them.

148
00:07:53,170 --> 00:07:58,108
On July 11th, after seventy-nine
days, the original came back as

149
00:07:58,108 --> 00:08:02,370
Coca-Cola Classic. In the two days
after that announcement, the

150
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:06,096
company got more than
thirty-one thousand calls.

151
00:08:06,096 --> 00:08:09,509
New Coke is remembered as one of the
biggest marketing mistakes

152
00:08:09,509 --> 00:08:13,833
ever. But it showed the company
something no taste test could:

153
00:08:13,833 --> 00:08:16,017
how much people cared.

154
00:08:18,945 --> 00:08:23,007
Today, the Coca-Cola system sells
more than thirty-three billion

155
00:08:23,007 --> 00:08:26,708
cases of drinks a year, in over
two hundred countries and

156
00:08:26,708 --> 00:08:31,583
territories. In 2024, the
company's revenue was about

157
00:08:31,583 --> 00:08:33,988
forty-seven billion dollars.

158
00:08:33,988 --> 00:08:37,050
And it still works a lot
like Candler's deal.

159
00:08:37,050 --> 00:08:41,025
The company mostly makes the
concentrate and builds the brand.

160
00:08:41,025 --> 00:08:44,438
Independent bottling partners turn
it into drinks and get them

161
00:08:44,438 --> 00:08:45,790
to stores.

162
00:08:45,790 --> 00:08:48,965
Look back at the whole story, and
the recipe was never the whole

163
00:08:48,965 --> 00:08:52,503
secret. Free samples got people to try it.

164
00:08:52,503 --> 00:08:55,577
A one-dollar deal put it in every town.

165
00:08:55,577 --> 00:08:57,728
A bottle made it hard to fake.

166
00:08:57,728 --> 00:09:00,090
A war carried it around the world.

167
00:09:00,090 --> 00:09:03,190
And when the company tried to
change it, its own customers

168
00:09:03,190 --> 00:09:04,556
wouldn't let it.

169
00:09:04,556 --> 00:09:07,806
John Pemberton sold about
nine glasses a day.

170
00:09:07,806 --> 00:09:09,656
He thought he'd made a medicine.

171
00:09:09,656 --> 00:09:12,131
What he actually started was a habit...

172
00:09:12,131 --> 00:09:16,106
repeated two point two
billion times a day.
